{
  "requirement": "The SDK presents node inputs using the returned value's `NodeContents::format`\nunder {cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.format`.\nIts text and fingerprint describe the same retained observation.\n\n```rust,ignore\nlet contents = domain.node_content(&node_id)?;\nlet text = contents.format();\n```\n\nLoading and fallible preparation occur in `Domain::node_content` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content`. For inputs queued on an ask\nbuilder, these failures return `ValidationError::Graph`. During a `read_node`\ntool call, they are returned to the agent as a tool error, so it can continue\nand recover.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The structural obligation is delegation through a named interface. The text says \"The SDK presents node inputs using the returned value's `NodeContents::format`\". It also says \"Loading and fallible preparation occur in `Domain::node_content`\". An alternative could render inputs with `Debug` or a separate serializer, or could re-read the node after fingerprinting. That would keep the output similar but violate these clauses, so they are structural. The error-routing clauses (`ValidationError::Graph` for the ask builder, a tool error for `read_node`) only specify observable behavior.\n\nBoth supplied roots meet the obligation:\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs#impl[Ask<'a, 'd, D, R, P>]#2/fn[execute]` calls `ctx.prepare_read(&node)` for each queued input. It maps failures with `.map_err(ValidationError::Graph)?`, and it builds the input text from `snapshot.content.format()` (redaction is applied to that text afterwards). The fingerprint stored in `PreparedInput` is the snapshot's fingerprint.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tools.rs#impl[SessionTools<'a, D>]/fn[read_node]` calls `inspection.prepare_read(&node)` and renders `snapshot.content.format()`. It records the observation with `snapshot.fingerprint`. A failure becomes an `io::Error` returned from the tool function. Where the tool framework turns that into a tool error for the agent is observable behavior, not structure, so I did not trace it.\n\nBoth roots go through `crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs#impl[ValidationContext<'d, D>]#2/fn[prepare_read]`. It calls `self.graph.node_content(node)?` once, computes `content.fingerprint()` from that same value, and returns both together as a `Snapshot`. The text and fingerprint therefore describe the same retained observation. That helper is reached from both marked roots, so it needs no marker of its own.\n\nThe `NodeContents::format` signature and its \"no further reads\" property belong to `requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.format`, which is cited here, not refined here. I judged only the supplied text.\n\nbehavioral: The tests adequately cover `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.judge.rendering`.\n\n- **Rendering through `format`.** `src/agent/tests.rs#fn[default_tool_accepts_scalar_results_and_keeps_the_exact_prefix]` sets a custom formatter and asserts the user message sent to the agent contains \"formatted input: source\". The delivered text therefore comes from the returned value's `format`, not from a separate rendering path. `explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots` in `src/graph/validation_tests.rs` checks the order and repetition of delivered inputs. `behavioral_adequacy_uses_domain_contents_and_returns_a_typed_fingerprinted_answer` checks that domain contents appear in the message, in order. `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[domain_formats_supplied_contents_without_resolving_a_replacement]` checks that `format` uses only the supplied contents.\n- **Text and fingerprint describe the same observation.** `tests/contracts.rs#fn[identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot]` reads a snapshot, then changes the underlying contents. It asserts `format()` still returns \"before\", the fingerprint is unchanged and matches the snapshot, and the node was read only once. The agent tests also check that the recorded `Dependency::Node` fingerprint matches the content delivered to the agent (\"source\"). `agent_executions_isolate_conversations_and_dependency_traces` checks the same.\n- **Ask-builder failures return `ValidationError::Graph`.** `src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[queued_reads_selection_and_rendering_fail_before_agent_invocation]` forces a loading failure with `fail_read`. It asserts `Err(ValidationError::Graph(\"read\"))` for both current-node and selected-node inputs. It forces a preparation failure with `fail_prepare`. It asserts `Err(ValidationError::Graph(\"render\"))`. It also asserts that no dependencies are recorded and that the agent is not invoked.\n- **Failures in a `read_node` tool call reach the agent as a tool error.** `src/agent/tests.rs#fn[node_preparation_tool_errors_allow_recovery_without_recording_undelivered_contents]` makes `Domain::node_content` fail. It asserts the second request carries an error `ToolResult` for `read_node` with the message \"unsupported node contents\". It asserts the agent then submits a result, so it can continue and recover. It asserts no dependency is recorded. The test only exercises a preparation failure. A loading failure comes from the same `Domain::node_content` error return, and the ask-builder test covers both kinds. I treat this as covered and not a separate gap.\n\nThe requirement that formatting needs no further reads is the cited `requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.format`. It is not reassessed here, and `domain_formats_supplied_contents_without_resolving_a_replacement` covers it.",
  "references": "c977547238a81cb98391fb6925b1da84b1b40b993d321565952baffccf99dd68",
